    },
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "The artist, Paris; with Hermann Teubner (1824–1888), Philadelphia, by Apr. 1878 [Pennsylvania Academy of Fine Art 1878]; [returned to artist]; with Mrs. M. A. Mitchell, June 1878 [Massachusetts Charitable Mechanics Association 1878, 48; Mathews, ed. 1984, 138]; returned to the artist, after Oct. 1878; sold to John Ruderman, Montclair, NJ [this and the following according to Hirschl & Adler Galleries 1959, 8]. Lewis Padawer (1906–1975) and Rose Padawer (1906–2013; born Anolik), New York, by Jan. 1957. John J. Cunningham, New York, by Sept. 1959 [this and the following according to letter from Norman Hirschl, Feb. 13, 1979; copy in curatorial object file]; sold to Hirschl & Adler Galleries, New York, Sept. 1959; sold to Sterling Morton (1885–1961) and Sophia P. Morton (1890–1969; born Sophia Preston Owsley; also Mrs. Sterling Morton), Chicago, Oct. 26, 1959 [letter from Norman Hirschl, Nov. 21, 1978; copy in curatorial object file]; to Sophia Morton, Chicago, from 1961; bequeathed to the Art Institute of Chicago, 1969.",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435438",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Provenance Statement",
          "classified_as": [
            {
              "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300418049",
              "type": "Type",
              "_label": "Brief Text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "Mary Cassatt chose a quintessentially Spanish subject, executing this composition of a bullfighter, or <em>torero</em>, in full regalia during an extended stay in Seville. Having trained in Philadelphia and Paris, Cassatt ventured to Spain to study the country’s Renaissance and Baroque works and to follow the path of French avant-garde artists like Édouard Manet.",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026032",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Summary",
          "classified_as": [
            {
              "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300418049",
              "type": "Type",
              "_label": "Brief Text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "<p>During her first years as a professional artist, Cassatt traveled largely on her own to study European art, including an extended stay in Italy and Spain in 1872–73. She looked especially to Renaissance and Baroque art of the 15th to 17th centuries and to the avant-garde explorations of her French contemporaries for inspiration. Cassatt harmonized these various elements in <em>After the Bullfight</em>, executed in Seville: The figure’s controlled, tonal modeling recalls that of Spanish Baroque painter Diego Velázquez, and the dynamic flourishes of rich pigment describing the bullfighter’s regalia evoke the modernist techniques of Frenchman Édouard Manet.</p>\n",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Description",
          "classified_as": [
            {
              "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300418049",
              "type": "Type",
              "_label": "Brief Text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "format": "text/html"
    }
